From 80ac64f6f3f8a24e34cae5cbcd288c429263d09c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michiel <367462+meghuizen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 13:27:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] xfs: let stat() run ->getattr in rcu-walk

Set IOP_GETATTR_RCU in xfs_setup_iops(), which every xfs inode passes
through and where all four inode_operations tables, each with
xfs_vn_getattr(), are installed.

xfs_vn_getattr() reads the xfs_inode (size, block counts, crtime,
diflags, extent size hint), the xfs_mount (feature bits, block size,
stripe geometry, atomic write limits) and, for STATX_DIOALIGN and
STATX_WRITE_ATOMIC, the buftarg's block device limits. It takes no
lock and no reference, and its one call out is a tracepoint. So it
qualifies as it is; the helpers it uses - xfs_report_dioalign(),
xfs_report_atomic_write(), xfs_get_atomic_write_{min,max,max_opt}(),
xfs_stat_blksize() - are the same kind of reads.

xfs reuses inode memory: a reclaimed xfs_inode can be recycled for
another inode number by xfs_iget_recycle() without an RCU grace period,
so a ->getattr running in rcu-walk may read a half-reinitialised inode.
That is already true of the rcu-walk itself, which reads i_mode and
i_opflags the same way; the values are garbage but every pointer
followed - i_mount, the buftarg, the superblock - is stable for the
life of the mount, and the VFS discards the result because the dentry's
sequence count has moved.
---
 fs/xfs/xfs_iops.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fs/xfs/xfs_iops.c b/fs/xfs/xfs_iops.c
index d1306e723899..549c1d179475 100644
--- a/fs/xfs/xfs_iops.c
+++ b/fs/xfs/xfs_iops.c
@@ -1510,4 +1510,10 @@ xfs_setup_iops(
 		init_special_inode(inode, inode->i_mode, inode->i_rdev);
 		break;
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Every table above uses xfs_vn_getattr(), which only reads the
+	 * inode, the mount and the buftarg limits. See the flag's definition.
+	 */
+	inode->i_opflags |= IOP_GETATTR_RCU;
 }
